# Generate Sidekiq Worker

Create a new Sidekiq worker (job class + test) that looks like it was written
by this project's team, not by a template.

## Workflow

1. Determine the worker name from `$ARGUMENTS` or the conversation; ask if
   neither settles it.
2. **Discover the project's conventions before writing any code.** Glob for
   existing workers (`app/workers/**/*.rb`, `app/sidekiq/**/*.rb`,
   `app/jobs/**/*.rb`<% if any_gem?("packs-rails", "packwerk") %>, `packs/**/app/workers/**/*.rb`, `packs/**/app/jobs/**/*.rb`<% end %>)
   and read 2–3 recent ones. Note and follow:
   - The base: `include Sidekiq::Job`, an `ApplicationWorker`/`ApplicationJob`
     parent, or project-specific SLA modules — use whatever the project uses.
   - Queue names actually in use (check `config/sidekiq.yml` too) and how
     workers pick them.
   - Extra `sidekiq_options` the project sets consistently (e.g. an
     ownership/team tag for alerting, explicit `retry:`) — carry the
     convention forward.
   - Namespacing and file placement.
3. Choose the queue by SLA. Prefer the project's own queues; only if the
   project has no clear layout, fall back to the standard latency-based
   split from the `martian-sidekiq` skill (`critical` / `default` / `low`).
4. Create the worker file. Whatever the base looks like, `perform` must:
   - take IDs and primitives only (no AR objects, symbols, or Time objects),
   - start with early-exit guards (record gone / already processed), so the
     job is idempotent — Sidekiq retries are at-least-once,
   - set an explicit `retry:` when the default 25 (~21 days) doesn't match
     the work's useful lifetime.
5. Create the test file mirroring the worker's path<% if gem?("rspec-rails") %> (`spec/workers/...`)<% end %>.
6. Show where and how the worker gets enqueued. If the enqueue happens inside
   a DB transaction, do NOT call `perform_async` there — move it after the
   transaction or into an `after_commit` (see the skill's "Enqueue inside a
   transaction").
<%- if any_gem?("sorbet", "sorbet-static-and-runtime", "tapioca") -%>
7. Regenerate the Sorbet RBI for the new class:
   `bin/tapioca dsl FullyQualified::WorkerClassName`. If RBI files change,
   they belong in the same commit as the worker.
<%- end -%>

## Fallback worker template

Use only the parts the project's own conventions don't already dictate:

```ruby
# frozen_string_literal: true

class ${WorkerName}
  include Sidekiq::Job

  sidekiq_options queue: :default, retry: 10

  def perform(record_id)
    record = SomeModel.find_by(id: record_id)
    return unless record            # deleted between enqueue and run
    return if record.processed?     # idempotency guard

    # work
  end
end
```
<%- if gem?("rspec-rails") -%>

## Fallback spec template

```ruby
# frozen_string_literal: true

RSpec.describe ${WorkerName} do
  describe "#perform" do
    it "processes the record" do
      record = create(:some_factory)

      described_class.new.perform(record.id)

      expect(record.reload).to have_attributes(
        # expected state after processing
      )
    end

    it "no-ops when the record is gone" do
      expect { described_class.new.perform(-1) }.not_to raise_error
    end

    it "no-ops when already processed" do
      record = create(:some_factory, :processed)

      expect { described_class.new.perform(record.id) }
        .not_to change { record.reload.updated_at }
    end
  end
end
```
<%- end -%>

## Pre-merge checklist

- [ ] Running `perform` twice with the same args produces the same end state
- [ ] Arguments are IDs and primitives, well under 1 KB
- [ ] No enqueue inside an open DB transaction
- [ ] `retry:` matches the work's useful lifetime
- [ ] The job completes in minutes, not tens of minutes — split long work
      into `perform_bulk` child jobs

Consult the `martian-sidekiq` skill for queue layout, retry/backoff design,
uniqueness locks, and the full idempotency strategies table.
